{{Short description|British-bred Thoroughbred racehorse}}

'''Spanker''' (foaled 1678),<ref name=TBH/> sometimes called '''Old Spanker''' or the '''Pelham Arabian''', was a British [[Thoroughbred]] [[racehorse]] who sired many important horses in the history of the breed.  He was said to be the best horse in [[Newmarket, Suffolk|Newmarket]], the home of British racing, during the reign of [[Charles II of England|Charles II]].<ref name=TBH/>

==History==

Spanker was a [[Bay (horse)|bay]] horse.  His [[sire]] and [[dam]] were [[D'Arcy Yellow Turk]] and the ''Old Morocco Mare'' (by [[Fairfax Morocco Barb]]). He was bred at Brocklesby, [[Lincolnshire]] by Charles Pelham,{{sfn|Whyte|1840|p=423}} although some accounts have him bred by [[George Villiers, 2nd Duke of Buckingham]] and only later passing into Pelham's hands.<ref name=TBH/>

He went on to sire some important horses in the breed including [[Careless (horse)|Careless]], [[St. Martin (horse)|St. Martin]], and [[Young Spanker]], who went on to sire the dam of [[Basto (horse)|Basto]].<ref name=TBH/>

